      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to calculate the product \\(\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\\). We can start by observing the pattern in the numerators and denominators.\n\nThe product can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nThis can be simplified by canceling out common terms in the numerator and the denominator. Notice that:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50 = 50!\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53 = 53! / 4!\n\\]\n\nSo the product becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{50!}{\\frac{53!}{4!}} = \\frac{50! \\cdot 4!}{53!} = \\frac{4!}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50!} = \\frac{24}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}\n\\]\n\nNow we can calculate the value of the product:\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51} = \\frac{24}{140412} = \\frac{2}{11685}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{11685}}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculation is accurate.\n```python\nfrom math import factorial\n\n# Calculate the numerator of the product\nnumerator = factorial(50)\n\n# Calculate the denominator of the product\ndenominator = factorial(53) // factorial(4)\n\n# Calculate the final product\nproduct = numerator // denominator\n\n# Express the final answer as a fraction\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nfinal_fraction = Fraction(product).limit_denominator()\n\nprint(final_fraction)\n```",
