      "text": "The given expression can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\nWe can rewrite each term in the numerator and denominator as follows:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\nNotice that each numerator is divisible by the corresponding denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{1 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\nWe can cancel out the common terms:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{1 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\nNext, we observe that the denominator is a product of all integers from 1 to 53, and the numerator is a product of all integers from 2 to 50. We can simplify this further by recognizing the telescoping nature of the product:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53} = \\frac{50}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50}\n\\]\nThe \\(50\\) in the numerator and denominator cancel out:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}\n\\]\nThus, the simplified form of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{140452}}\n\\]\n\nLet s be the sum of the digits of the number } 11^3 + 11^4. \\text{ What is the value of s?} \\text{\n\nTo find the sum of the digits of the number \\(11^3 + 11^4\\), we first need to calculate \\(11^3\\) and \\(11^4\\).\n\nStarting with \\(11^3\\):\n\\[\n11^3 = 11 \\times 11 \\times 11 = 121 \\times 11 = 1331\n\\]\nNext, we calculate \\(11^4\\):\n\\[\n11^4 = 11 \\times 11^3 = 11 \\times 1331 = 14641\n\\]\nNow, we add these two results together:\n\\[\n11^3 + 11^4 = 1331 + 14641 = 15972\n\\]\nNext, we find the sum of the digits of 15972. The digits are 1, 5, 9, 7, and 2. Adding these together, we get:\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 9 + 7 + 2 = 24\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(s\\) is \\(\\boxed{24}\\).\n\nIf ${f'}\\left(1\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}$, then $\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to 0}\\frac{{f({1+\\Delta x})-f(1)}}{{2\\Delta x}}$=____.\n\nTo solve the given limit \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{2 \\Delta x}\\) given that \\(f'(1) = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we can use the definition of the derivative. Recall that the derivative of \\(f\\) at \\(x = 1\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ f'(1) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{\\Delta x} \\]\n\nWe are given that \\(f'(1) = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Therefore, we can substitute this value into the definition of the derivative:\n\n\\[ \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of the given limit:\n\n\\[ \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{2 \\Delta x} \\]\n\nNotice that the expression inside the limit can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{2 \\Delta x} = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{\\Delta x} \\right) \\]\n\nSince we know that \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we can substitute this value in:\n\n\\[ \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{f(1 + \\Delta x) - f(1)}{\\Delta x} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the given limit is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\]",
