      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the product of the sequence $\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}$. Let's write out the product step by step:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nWe can express this product as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nNotice that the numerator is the product of the first 50 positive integers, and the denominator is the product of the integers from 4 to 53. Notice also that many terms will cancel out. Let's write the numerator and denominator more clearly:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange the factors in the denominator to make cancellation easier:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{(4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 51 \\cdot 52) \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's look at the range of the remaining factors in the numerator and the denominator. The numerator has the factor 1, 2, 3, ..., 49, 50. The denominator starts with 4, 5, 6, ..., 51, 52, and ends with 53. We can see that most of the factors in the numerator will be canceled by corresponding factors in the denominator. Specifically, the numerator will have 50 factors, and the denominator will have 51 factors, but the factor 53 in the denominator will not be canceled out by any factor in the numerator.\n\nSo the product simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{50}{53}}\n\\]",
